<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Star-Spangled Banner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">poem written 14 September <a href="/years/1814">1814</a>; printed as a broadside 17 September <a href="/years/1814">1814</a>; national anthem by law in <a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a>, after the burning of Washington, British forces arrested William Beanes, a physician of Upper Marlboro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, and a friend of the lawyer Francis Scott Key. With the sanction of President Madison, Key went to Baltimore in early September 1814 and, with John Stuart Skinner, an American agent for prisoners of war, sailed down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a> to the British fleet.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Australian Broadcasting Commission used it to introduce its radio news from 1942 to 1952, and towards the end of the Second World War some cinemas played it alongside &quot;God Save the King&quot; and &quot;<b>The Star-Spangled Banner</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/advance-australia-fair">Advance Australia Fair</a></li><li><blockquote>At various times, above all during the presidency of John F. Kennedy, there have been efforts to make it a national hymn or anthem beside or in place of &quot;<b>The Star-Spangled Banner</b>&quot;, and before 1931 it was considered a candidate for the national anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/america-the-beautiful">America the Beautiful</a></li><li><blockquote>Drawing on &quot;God Save the King&quot;, &quot;<b>The Star-Spangled Banner</b>&quot;, the national anthem of Russia, the &quot;Battle Hymn of the Republic&quot; and Vivaldi's Gloria, he spent two months on the melody, working backwards from its last line, which gives the song its title.</blockquote><a href="/songs/glory-to-hong-kong">Glory to Hong Kong</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1978 Geraint Jarman a'r Cynganeddwyr recorded a version with electric guitars, played by Tich Gwilym and inspired by Jimi Hendrix's &quot;<b>The Star-Spangled Banner</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hen-wlad-fy-nhadau">Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the 1952 law, &quot;La Borinqueña&quot; and &quot;<b>The Star-Spangled Banner</b>&quot; are both played at official events, while at international sports competitions such as the Olympics only &quot;La Borinqueña&quot; is played.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-borinquena">La Borinqueña</a></li><li><blockquote>The name has been criticized: Timothy Askew of Clark Atlanta University argued that it could suggest a wish for separatism, though the words name no race, and other commentators have called it a slight to &quot;<b>The Star-Spangled Banner</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lift-every-voice-and-sing">Lift Every Voice and Sing</a></li></ul></section>
